1
00:00:00,840 --> 00:00:06,140
Let's move now to the disk structure. How
is the physical disk organized in order to

2
00:00:06,140 --> 00:00:12,990
maintain one or more filesystems? So before
we start with this,

3
00:00:12,990 --> 00:00:15,500
think about the following question:

4
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:23,529
You have your laptop and it's currently turned
off. You know you have the disk. What is persisted

5
00:00:23,529 --> 00:00:28,520
on the disk is there and you can of course
read it and write it. And now you know that

6
00:00:28,520 --> 00:00:31,480
you actually read and write in blocks.

7
00:00:31,480 --> 00:00:34,820
And then you have your main memory, but that
is volatile memory, so since the computer

8
00:00:34,820 --> 00:00:41,050
is turned off for what we know, it's empty.
Now you know that you turn the computer on

9
00:00:41,050 --> 00:00:47,010
and magically your operating system starts
and then you can run your processes and so on.

10
00:00:47,010 --> 00:00:48,710
So the question is:

11
00:00:48,710 --> 00:00:54,879
How do we do that? so we know that we will
need to read for sure something from the disk

12
00:00:54,879 --> 00:01:00,010
and bring it into memory so that we start
the processes and the very first process that will

13
00:01:00,010 --> 00:01:05,630
start is the one that will
actually eventually lead to the operating

14
00:01:05,630 --> 00:01:07,789
system

15
00:01:07,789 --> 00:01:12,329
being the process that runs, like we saw this
when we discussed processes with the init

16
00:01:12,329 --> 00:01:19,549
process. But how is this actually physically
done? Well, the answer to that is that

17
00:01:19,549 --> 00:01:26,789
again, this is collaboration between the hardware
and the operating system. There is an agreement

18
00:01:26,789 --> 00:01:32,450
about how things are stored on the disk
so that when you turn on the computer, the

19
00:01:32,450 --> 00:01:33,969
hardware

20
00:01:33,969 --> 00:01:40,279
can go and take from the disk things and start
the operating system. Because as we just said,

21
00:01:40,279 --> 00:01:45,560
when the computer is off, volatile memory /
main memory is basically empty, so where is

22
00:01:45,560 --> 00:01:49,890
the operating system? How does the operating
system know what to do given that it does

23
00:01:49,890 --> 00:01:57,380
not exist yet? So this starts based on how
your disk is organized. There is a fixed structure

24
00:01:57,380 --> 00:02:03,969
for the disk and the hardware is aware of that
and it uses such knowledge to actually boot

25
00:02:03,969 --> 00:02:05,159
your computer.

26
00:02:05,159 --> 00:02:11,349
So this is how it looks. You have partitions
and each partition is basically a file system

27
00:02:14,480 --> 00:02:19,180
Inside each partition we will see you have

28
00:02:19,180 --> 00:02:24,459
the initial part that is about the specific type
of file system and the rest that is actually

29
00:02:24,459 --> 00:02:29,969
dependent on what the users are doing with
that file system.

30
00:02:29,969 --> 00:02:35,060
But we also have something called the master
boot record and the agreement between the hardware

31
00:02:35,060 --> 00:02:40,319
and the operating system, and in this case
also the disk is that

32
00:02:40,319 --> 00:02:46,549
this exists at the very beginning of the disk.
OK, it starts at sector 0. If you have a rotating

33
00:02:46,549 --> 00:02:50,299
disk or at another address, but there is an
agreement about where it starts. Let's say

34
00:02:50,299 --> 00:02:56,180
it starts at 0 in this case, and what you
put there is the minimal

35
00:02:56,180 --> 00:03:04,299
set of instructions that the hardware knows
it needs to read, put in memory and start executing,

36
00:03:04,299 --> 00:03:10,129
and that set of instructions are going to
be the ones that decide which file system

37
00:03:10,129 --> 00:03:16,400
to load, so inside which partition to go,
and based on that which files to load to actually

38
00:03:16,400 --> 00:03:19,810
start the operating system

39
00:03:19,810 --> 00:03:24,159
The partition table, so the information about
all the partitions is found at the end of the

40
00:03:24,159 --> 00:03:26,459
master

41
00:03:26,459 --> 00:03:33,459
boot record and again it's used by the code
that you store in the master boot record to

42
00:03:33,459 --> 00:03:37,620
actually decide which partition to run. So
if you have a computer and you have multiple

43
00:03:37,620 --> 00:03:41,959
operating systems, it means that you have multiple
most likely partitions on each partition as

44
00:03:41,959 --> 00:03:49,280
a different file system, and what you put
in the master boot record is a set of instructions

45
00:03:49,280 --> 00:03:54,200
that will interact with the user asking the
user which system do you want to start, and

46
00:03:54,200 --> 00:03:59,569
based on that, decide into which file system
to go and which things to load to actually

47
00:03:59,569 --> 00:04:03,150
wake up the operating system.

48
00:04:03,150 --> 00:04:08,569
This again, it's done in a similar way, so
basically at the very beginning of each partition

49
00:04:08,569 --> 00:04:12,939
you have the boot block. There you will put
again the stuff that the hardware needs to

50
00:04:12,939 --> 00:04:18,780
load into memory and start executing to actually
start the operating system, so that is

51
00:04:18,780 --> 00:04:24,800
where you will put the operating system. Maybe
not all of it, but at least the minimum set

52
00:04:24,800 --> 00:04:28,860
of instructions you need to wake the operating
system up and then let the operating system

53
00:04:28,860 --> 00:04:33,240
go on with the rest of tasks.

54
00:04:33,240 --> 00:04:38,120
This is what I said before. After the boot
block usually have this so called Super Block

55
00:04:38,120 --> 00:04:42,889
which contains information that is needed
by the operating system. But now it's specific

56
00:04:42,889 --> 00:04:46,910
to the operating system you are trying to
load and to the file system that you use to

57
00:04:46,910 --> 00:04:51,240
maintain the information of the operating
system and after that you will have extra

58
00:04:51,240 --> 00:04:58,150
things, but these usually depend on what
the user is storing on the

59
00:04:58,150 --> 00:05:04,020
file system, the files, what space is free,
which again of course is a function of what

60
00:05:04,020 --> 00:05:06,500
the user is doing with the file system, and
so on and so forth.

